<p>No laptop. No notifications. A paper map, a fountain pen, and a camera that does not know the internet exists. I expected to feel restless. Instead, I felt something closer to relief.</p>

<p>The strange thing about leaving your tools behind is how quickly your sense of time changes. An hour becomes an hour. A walk becomes a walk. You stop reaching for the thing that turns every pause into a task.</p>

<p>I wrote down small observations in a notebook: the particular green of the water, a dog waiting outside a bakery, the way the light changed at four o’clock. None of it was important. All of it was the point.</p>

<p>I don’t think the answer is to become a person who lives offline. I like my work too much. I like the people I meet through it. But I do think we need periods when the input stops and the world becomes local again.</p>

<p>Monday morning, I opened my laptop and felt the familiar pull of the inbox. But underneath it was a little more room. The weekend had done its quiet work.</p>
